 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Hash Collisions:** The performance of hash encoding depends on the hash function and table size. Collisions can lead to artifacts [G].
-*   **SDF Thresholding:** The choice of threshold for eliminating invalid points affects the quality of the reconstructed geometry [S1].
-*   **Textureless Areas:** The method performs better than baselines in textureless areas, but poor initialization or insufficient training can still lead to artifacts [S1].
+*   **Hash Collisions:** With a limited hash table size, collisions can occur. The source uses 16 layers, which helps mitigate this, but implementation must handle hash table resizing or collision resolution [S1].
+*   **SDF Thresholding:** Using the mean SDF as a threshold for invalid points can be sensitive to noise. If the scene is not centered or the bounding box is poorly chosen, the mean may not represent the surface accurately [S1].
+*   **Initialization:** The method relies on a modified initialization strategy from Ref [12]. Incorrect initialization can lead to poor convergence [S1].
+*   **Resolution:** The marching cubes extraction is fixed at $512^3$. Lower resolutions may lose detail, while higher resolutions increase memory and computation time [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Training time is 1.8 hours for the proposed method, compared to 5.2 hours for IDR and 7.2 hours for NeuS [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage is 5 GB for the proposed method, compared to 6.5 GB for IDR and 7 GB for NeuS [S1].
-*   **Performance:** The method achieves a mean Chamfer-L2 distance of $0.72 \times 10^{-6}$ and a mean PSNR of 30.85 dB on the DTU dataset [S1].
-*   **Rays per Iteration:** 512 rays per iteration [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Reconstructs more geometric detail than baselines like NeuS, IDR, VolSDF, and UNISURF, especially in textureless areas [S1]. Provides better high-frequency detail than NeRF [S1].
